[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Circular Linked Lists"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"A circular linked list is a type of linked list where the last node points back to the first node, forming a circle. Unlike a singly or doubly linked list, there is no 'end' to a circular linked list. This structure can be useful in scenarios where you need to repeatedly cycle through a set of elements."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Types of Circular Linked Lists"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Singly Circular Linked List: Each node contains data and a pointer to the next node in the list. The last node points to the first node."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Doubly Circular Linked List: Each node contains data, a pointer to the next node, and a pointer to the previous node. The last node's 'next' pointer points to the first node, and the first node's 'previous' pointer points to the last node."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Operations on Circular Linked Lists"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Common operations performed on circular linked lists include insertion, deletion, traversal, and searching. These operations need to be handled carefully to maintain the circular structure."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Insertion"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Insertion can be done at the beginning, end, or at a specific position in the list. When inserting at the beginning or end, special care must be taken to update the pointers of the first and last nodes to maintain the circular property."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// Inserting a node at the beginning of a singly circular linked list (Java example)
Node newNode = new Node(data);
if (head == null) {
    newNode.next = newNode;
    head = newNode;
} else {
    Node temp = head;
    while (temp.next != head) {
        temp = temp.next;
    }
    temp.next = newNode;
    newNode.next = head;
    head = newNode;
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Deletion"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Deletion involves removing a node from the list. Similar to insertion, deleting the first or last node requires updating the pointers of adjacent nodes to preserve the circular structure. Deletion at a specific position requires traversing the list to find the node to be deleted."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Deleting a node with a specific value from a singly circular linked list (Java example)
if (head == null) return;
Node curr = head, prev = null;

if (curr.data == key) {
    prev = head;
    while (prev.next != head)
        prev = prev.next;
    head = head.next;
    prev.next = head;
    return;
}

while (curr.next != head && curr.data != key) {
    prev = curr;
    curr = curr.next;
}

if (curr.data == key) {
    prev.next = curr.next;
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Traversal"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traversing a circular linked list involves visiting each node in the list. Since there is no 'end', a condition must be set to stop the traversal, typically when the traversal returns to the starting node (head)."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// Traversing a singly circular linked list (Java example)
Node temp = head;
if (head != null) {
    do {
        System.out.println(temp.data);
        temp = temp.next;
    } while (temp != head);
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Advantages of Circular Linked Lists"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Efficient for implementing queues: Can easily implement a queue by maintaining a pointer to the rear node."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Useful in resource management: Can be used to represent a circular buffer or a round-robin scheduling algorithm."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No null pointers: Eliminates the need to check for null pointers when traversing the list."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Disadvantages of Circular Linked Lists"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"More complex implementation: Requires careful handling of pointers to maintain the circular structure."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Potential for infinite loops: If the circular property is not maintained correctly, it can lead to infinite loops during traversal."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Applications"},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Round-robin scheduling: Used in operating systems to allocate CPU time to processes in a circular manner."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Music playlists: Can be used to create a playlist that loops continuously."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Multiplayer games: Can represent a circle of players in a game."}]
